## Why This Is Asked

Assess the candidate's ability to design per-tenant fairness in a high-throughput KCNA pipeline, balancing isolation, latency, and dynamic scaling under bursty traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant token-bucket quotas and fair queuing
- Backpressure signaling (429s, retry-after) and quota persistence
- Telemetry-driven dynamic rebalancing and burst forgiveness
- Canary-based validation and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryConsume(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test dynamic quota rebalancing under tenant churn?
- How do you prevent misbehaving tenants from starving others while preserving low latency?

## Why This Is Asked

Leverages real-world needs for privacy-preserving analytics in a multi-tenant KCNA deployment, including DP budgeting, auditability, and safe exposure of aggregates.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant isolation via topic prefixes and ACLs
- Differential privacy budgeting and noise mechanisms
- Streaming topology with processors and backpressure
- Tamper-resistance via cryptographic proofs (hash chains, Merkle) and audit logs
- Canary rollout and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
function addLaplaceNoise(value, epsilon, sensitivity){
  const rnd = Math.random() - 0.5;
  const scale = sensitivity / epsilon;
  // simple Laplace sample using inverse CDF
  const noise = -scale * Math.sign(rnd) * Math.log(1 - 2 * Math.abs(rnd));
  return value + noise;
}
```

